---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const ebbinghausPlot = {
  points: [{
    points: [[1, 44], [9, 36], [24, 33], [48, 28], [144, 25], [744, 21]],
    label: 'retention after learning nonsense syllables', mode: 'line', color: '#b45309',
  }],
  x: { min: 0, max: 760, label: 'hours after learning' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 100, label: 'retention (%)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const memoryCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · sensory register', note: 'Everything your senses deliver lands here first — enormous capacity, but it decays in well under a couple of seconds unless you attend to it. Most of it is simply overwritten. Attention is the gatekeeper of all later learning.' },
    { math: '2 · working memory: about 7±2 items', note: 'What you attend to enters working memory, the conscious workspace (Miller, 1956). It holds only a handful of items — or fewer if the items are unfamiliar — and loses them within seconds without rehearsal. This bottleneck explains why a long explanation can be "understood" and instantly gone.' },
    { math: '3 · encoding: meaning and chunking', note: 'Items survive by being encoded: organised into chunks, connected to what is already known, given meaning. A 10-digit number is 10 strangers; a phone number is a chunk. Encoding depth — deep, semantic processing — predicts how much reaches long-term memory.' },
    { math: '4 · long-term memory and schema', note: 'Encoded material is stored in long-term memory, woven into schemas — mental frameworks that organise knowledge. Schemas make experts efficient: a chess master does not remember 32 pieces, but a few meaningful patterns.' },
    { math: '5 · retrieval: cues and spacing', note: 'Learning is not storage; it is the ability to retrieve. Retrieval depends on cues matching how material was encoded, and on practice that is spaced and effortful (recall beats re-reading). Every successful retrieval re-entrenches the memory.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/education/foundational/the-learning-process">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Chapters 1 and 2 of Seifert & Sutton's <em>Educational Psychology</em> set the foundation
    for the whole field: chapter 1 establishes teaching as a research-informed profession —
    one that must keep reflecting on its own practice — and chapter 2 asks the central
    question, <strong>what actually happens when someone learns?</strong> Two grand answers
    dominate. The <em>behaviourist</em> view treats learning as a change in observable
    behaviour shaped by association and consequence. The <em>cognitive</em> view opens the
    black box: learning is a change in knowledge and mental structures. Every teaching
    decision you will ever make quietly assumes one of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Behaviourism: association and consequence</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Classical conditioning (Pavlov):</strong> a neutral signal paired repeatedly with something meaningful acquires its power. In classrooms this is the emotional weather — a subject paired with anxiety or with curiosity carries that pairing with it.</li>
    <li><strong>Operant conditioning (Skinner):</strong> behaviour is shaped by its consequences. <em>Positive</em> reinforcement adds something desirable after a behaviour; <em>negative</em> reinforcement removes something unpleasant — and both make the behaviour more likely. Punishment suppresses behaviour but teaches nothing about what to do instead.</li>
    <li><strong>The classroom takeaway:</strong> immediate, consistent consequences and the promise of reinforcement still explain a great deal of habit, routine and classroom management — while telling you little about understanding.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Behaviourism earned its influence by being measurable: define the behaviour, deliver the
    consequence, watch the behaviour change. Its blind spot is equally instructive. A pupil
    can emit the correct response for the wrong reason, and two pupils responding
    identically may hold completely different understandings — differences behaviourism
    cannot even express, because everything unobservable was out of scope. That limitation
    is precisely what drove the cognitive turn.
  </p>

  <h2>Cognitivism: the memory pipeline</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="sensory register → working memory → long-term memory — where learning lives or dies" widget="equationStep" config={memoryCfg} />
  <p>
    The pipeline model, the backbone of the cognitive view in chapter 2, converts vague
    advice into design: <em>attention</em> is a limited resource you must direct;
    <em>working memory</em> is a bottleneck you must respect (chunk, sequence, don't
    overload); <em>encoding</em> is what you build with meaning and schema; <em>retrieval</em>
    is the skill that makes learning stick. Metacognition — knowing what you know and how
    you learn — supervises the whole pipeline, which is why teaching learners to plan,
    monitor and evaluate their own understanding is itself a learning strategy.
  </p>

  <h2>The forgetting curve is brutal — and bendable</h2>
  <Widget no="2" caption="Ebbinghaus (1885): retention of learned material over time (1 hour to 744 hours)" widget="plotter" config={ebbinghausPlot} />
  <p>
    In 1885 Hermann Ebbinghaus memorised nonsense syllables and tested himself at intervals.
    Within <strong>20 minutes he had lost roughly 42%</strong> (retention ~58% — the first,
    steepest point, off this chart's scale); an hour later 44% survived; a day later 33%;
    after a month only about 21%. The plotted line is the original data. Its slope is not a
    law of nature but a warning: <em>without consolidation and rehearsal, most of what is
    learned today is gone tomorrow.</em> Spaced review, retrieval practice and meaningful
    encoding are the levers that flatten it — the empirical bridge between chapter 2's
    memory model and everything chapter 1's reflective teacher does on Monday morning.
  </p>

  <p>
    Chapter 1 frames all of this professionally: teaching is a profession of constant
    <strong>decision-making</strong> — what to teach, to whom, in what order, and what to do
    when it fails — and a professional is someone who justifies those decisions with
    evidence rather than habit. The chapters' stance is that teachers should read research
    the way physicians read trials: as grounds for reflective practice, revising technique
    when results disappoint. That stance is what turns the theory in this lesson from
    trivia into a working toolkit.
  </p>

  <h2>What the two views share</h2>
  <p>
    Behaviourism says: engineer the environment and consequences. Cognitivism says: engineer
    attention, encoding and retrieval. Neither is "wrong" — they answer different questions,
    and the professional stance of chapter 1 is to know which problem you are solving.
    Learning, in both views, is <em>a durable change produced by experience</em>; the
    argument is only over where to look for it.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Seifert & Sutton, Educational Psychology (2nd ed.), ch. 1–2 (Open Textbook Library); Ebbinghaus, Über das Gedächtnis (1885); Miller, "The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two", Psychological Review (1956).</p>
</TopicPage>
